# C / C++ testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to GoogleTest (and
Catch2, where a project already uses it) plus sanitizer-backed verification.
Applies to both C and C++ test files.

## Layout and naming

- Tests live in the project's existing test tree (`tests/`, `test/`, or
  beside the source with a `_test.cpp` suffix); do not invent a second
  convention.
- `TEST(SuiteName, CaseName)` standalone; `TEST_F(FixtureName, CaseName)` for
  shared `SetUp()`/`TearDown()` state. Name for the behavior, not
  `Test1`/`Test2`.
- `TEST_P` with `INSTANTIATE_TEST_SUITE_P` for a case run against a range of
  inputs.

## Fixtures and lifecycle

- Per-test setup that can fail in `SetUp()`, teardown in `TearDown()`, not
  the fixture's constructor/destructor.
- A fixture owning a resource (temp file, mock server, thread) releases it in
  `TearDown()` even when the body fails partway.

## Assertions

- `ASSERT_*` when the test cannot continue after a failure; `EXPECT_*` when
  later checks still give information.
- `ASSERT_DEATH`/`EXPECT_DEATH` for code whose contract is to terminate; set
  `GTEST_FLAG_SET(death_test_style, "threadsafe")` (or the project's style)
  when the binary is multi-threaded.
- `EXPECT_NEAR`/`EXPECT_FLOAT_EQ` for floating point, not `EXPECT_EQ`, unless
  the value is provably exact.

## Determinism and concurrency

- Never synchronize a multi-threaded test with `sleep`/`usleep`/
  `std::this_thread::sleep_for`; join, wait on a condition variable, or use a
  future/promise.
- A test of concurrent code is run under ThreadSanitizer
  (`-fsanitize=thread`) at least once.

## Sanitizer-backed verification

- Run the suite under AddressSanitizer + UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer for any
  change touching manual memory management, buffers, or pointer arithmetic.
- New behavior gets a new test in the same change; a bug fix (especially
  memory-safety or UB) gets a regression test that reproduces the failure
  under the relevant sanitizer before the fix and passes clean after.

## Fuzzing

- A parser or decoder of untrusted input formats gets a libFuzzer harness
  (`LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput`, `-fsanitize=fuzzer,address,undefined`) when the
  project has fuzzing infrastructure, or a documented seed corpus ready for
  one.
